# Ciele scheduled jobs: the same routes as vercel.json, on the same UTC
# schedules, so a self-host runs the same maintenance the hosted deployment
# does. Keep this file and vercel.json in step; deploy/compose.test.mjs fails
# if the two disagree about which jobs exist.
#
# Two schedules deliberately differ, and the test knows them by name. The
# durable worker can run every five minutes here; Vercel Hobby permits daily
# cron only. See RUN_ROUTINES below for the other exception.
#
# min hour dom mon dow  command
0 2 * * *   /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/rollup-usage
30 2 * * *  /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/sweep-retention
45 2 * * *  /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/security-detections
*/5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/finalize-crawls
# RUN_ROUTINES: hourly here, daily in vercel.json, and the difference is a
# platform limit rather than a decision. A Routine carries a preferred hour
# (#772), which only means something if the drain ticks more often than once a
# day: on a daily tick every routine runs at the tick's hour, whatever its
# author chose. Ordinary cron has no opinion about frequency, so a self-host
# honours the hour. Vercel's Hobby plan refuses any schedule that runs more
# than once a day, and the whole deployment fails rather than degrading, so the
# hosted file asks for daily and the hour is approximate there until the plan
# is Pro. Making the hosted one hourly is a one-line change in vercel.json.
0 * * * *   /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/run-routines
# RUN_REVIEWS (#841): hourly here for the same reason as routines, a review's
# timeout is measured in hours; daily on Vercel's Hobby plan.
15 * * * *  /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/run-reviews
35 * * * *  /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/run-webhooks
0 4 * * *   /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/verify-goals
30 4 * * *  /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/learn-graph
0 5 * * *   /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/run-exports
0 6 * * *   /usr/local/bin/run-job.sh /api/cron/sweep-recrawls
